---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout title="Strategic Theory - Replenish Guide">
    <PageHeader
        title="Strategic Theory"
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/decree.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Decree of Silence"
        imageCaption="Decree of Silence by Adam Rex"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Strategic Frameworks" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">The Two Angles Framework</h3>
        <p>
            This deck handles one angle of attack really well. Pure aggression,
            pure mana denial, pure countermagic. You can deal with all of those.
            Where it gets hard is when a deck attacks from two angles at the
            same time. A fast clock plus Wasteland and Port. Aggro backed by
            counterspells. If they're hitting you from two directions, you need
            to draw the right half of your answers, and with your card filtering
            starting at three mana, you can't fix a bad split fast enough.
        </p>
        <p>
            This is what should guide your mulligan decisions against the harder
            matchups. You need hands that have both early interaction and a path
            to your engines. All combo no defense doesn't work. All defense no
            engine doesn't work either.
        </p>

        <h3>Endpoint Play vs. Opportunistic Kills</h3>
        <p>
            Against control, think about the game in terms of endpoints. Imagine
            both players have drawn every card in their deck. If you match up
            your cards against their cards, who comes out ahead? If the answer
            is you, then don't throw away resources for marginal value. Don't
            activate Attunement unless you know exactly what you're pitching.
            Cast Frantic Search at end of their turn, not yours.
        </p>
        <p>
            Here's the thing: if they have eight counterspells and you have four
            Replenishes, four Opals, and three Decrees, your last Replenish is
            going to resolve. But only if you haven't wasted cards along the
            way.
        </p>
        <p>
            Post-board the dynamic changes. With Defense Grid and Mage and
            Angel, you can find windows for faster kills. Knowing when to shift
            from endpoint play to opportunistic play is one of the most
            important skills with this deck.
        </p>

        <h3>Attunement Discipline</h3>
        <p>
            I see a lot of players just spinning Attunement hoping something
            good happens. That can be wrong. Before you activate, ask yourself
            two questions: what cards am I willing to discard, and what am I
            looking for? If you can't answer both, don't activate. Against
            control especially, every reckless activation costs you. If you
            narrow your hand down looking for one specific thing and they deal
            with that thing, you've lost everything you discarded along the way.
        </p>
    </Card>
</Layout>
